        "id": "sec-agp218-3-1",
        "heading": "1. Soybean (Glycine max (L.) Merr.): Agronomy and Inoculation",
        "content": "Soybean, known as the 'Golden Bean' or 'Miracle Crop', is unique among grain legumes as it serves as both a premier oilseed (20% oil content) and a high-protein source (40-42% lysine-rich protein). Originating in Eastern Asia (China), soybean cultivation expanded dramatically in Central India (Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Rajasthan), designating Madhya Pradesh as the 'Soya State'.\n\n### Botanical and Agronomic Characteristics\n- **Growth Habit:** Determinate (stem terminates in a flower cluster; vegetative growth stops before flowering) vs Indeterminate (vegetative growth continues during flowering and pod development).\n- **Soil & Climate:** Well-drained fertile loam to deep black clay soils (Vertisols) with pH 6.5\u20137.5. Sensitive to waterlogging; raised bed or ridge-and-furrow planting is highly recommended.\n- **Seed Rate & Spacing:** Sowing rate of 65\u201375 kg/ha for small-medium seeded cultivars; spacing of 45 cm \u00d7 5\u201310 cm at a planting depth of 3\u20134 cm.\n- **Leading Cultivars:** JS 335, JS 93-05 (early maturing, 90\u201395 days), JS 95-60, NRC 37 (Ahilya 2), NRC 127, and DSb 21.\n\n### Inoculation and Nodulation Dynamics\nSoybean establishes a symbiotic relationship with *Bradyrhizobium japonicum*, an exclusive slow-growing rhizobial species not natively abundant in Indian soils outside traditional soybean tracts. Inoculation using lignite- or liquid-based *Bradyrhizobium japonicum* formulations at 500 g/ha or 10 ml/kg seed with 10% jaggery sticker solution is mandatory. Effective nodulation is marked by the internal pinkish-red coloration caused by leghemoglobin, which maintains an oxygen-free microenvironment for nitrogenase enzyme function.",
