        "heading": "2. Groundnut (Arachis hypogaea L.): Botany, Pegging, and Gypsum",
        "content": "Groundnut (peanut/monkey nut), native to South America (Brazil-Peru region), contains 45-50% high-quality edible oil and 25-28% digestible protein. It is predominantly cultivated in Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, and Rajasthan.\n\n### Botanical Classification\n- **Subspecies *fastigiata* (Bunch / Spanish-Valencia type):** Upright erect growth habit, non-spreading, clusters of pods around tap root, non-dormant seeds (dormancy absent; seeds germinate if rain occurs near maturity), early maturing (90\u2013110 days); e.g., TMV 2, JL 24, TAG 24, Kadiri 9.\n- **Subspecies *hypogaea* (Spreading / Virginia type):** Prostrate or trailing growth habit, pods scattered along trailing lateral branches, marked seed dormancy (requires 30-45 days rest), long duration (120\u2013140 days); e.g., TMV 1, M 13, Somnath, Girnar 2.\n\n### The Physiology of Pegging (Gynophore Elongation)\nFollowing self-pollination and fertilization, the base of the ovary elongates into a geotropic, needle-like stalk called the **peg** (gynophore). The peg curves downward and enters the soil (typically 3-5 cm deep). Soil darkness and mechanical resistance trigger horizontal orientation and subterranean pod enlargement.\n\n### Calcium Nutrition and Gypsum Application\nGroundnut pods absorb calcium directly from the surrounding soil solution through the shell wall, not via the root system or vascular xylem stream. Calcium deficiency prevents pod filling, resulting in blind or empty pods known as **'pops'**. Application of gypsum ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$) at 400-500 kg/ha at the flowering-to-pegging stage (35-45 DAS) provides soluble calcium ($Ca^{2+}$) in the pod zone and sulfur ($S$) for oil and amino acid synthesis.",
