        "heading": "3. Pigeonpea (Cajanus cajan (L.) Millsp.): Physiology and Cropping",
        "content": "Pigeonpea (Redgram / Arhar / Tur) is the second most crucial pulse crop in India after chickpea. Endemic to India, it is characterized by deep taproots (extending >2 meters), making it exceptionally drought-tolerant and a vital component of dryland cropping systems.\n\n### Plant Architecture and Maturity Groups\n- **Extra-Early & Early Maturing:** 100\u2013120 days (e.g., UPAS 120, Pusa 992, ICPL 87); fits well into Pigeonpea-Wheat crop rotation in northern irrigated belts.\n- **Medium Maturing:** 150\u2013180 days (e.g., Asha / ICPL 87119, BDN 711, Maruti / ICP 8863); standard rainfed crops in central and peninsular India.\n- **Late Maturing:** 200\u2013270 days (e.g., Bahar, NDA 1); traditional flood-prone or frost-free belts of Eastern UP and Bihar.\n\n### Hybrid Pigeonpea Technology\nICRISAT (Hyderabad) achieved a global breakthrough by releasing **ICPH 8** in 1991, the world's first commercial pulse hybrid based on genetic male sterility (GMS). Subsequent advancements developed Cytoplasmic-Genetic Male Sterility (CMS) using wild relatives (*Cajanus cajanifolius* - A4 cytoplasm), yielding superior hybrids like **ICPH 2671** and **ICPH 2740** that confer a 30-40% yield advantage over conventional inbreds.\n\n### Major Biotic Stresses\n- **Fusarium Wilt (*Fusarium udum*):** Soil-borne fungal disease causing xylem vascular browning, leaf withering, and complete plant death. Managed via resistant cultivars (Asha, Maruti), crop rotation with sorghum, and seed treatment with *Trichoderma viride*.\n- **Sterility Mosaic Disease (SMD):** Known as the 'green plague' because infected plants develop bushy, pale green leaves and complete cessation of flowering. Vectored by the eriophyid mite *Aceria cajani*. Resistant cultivars include ICPL 87119 (Asha) and BSMR 736.",
