      "modelAnswer": "Soybean (Glycine max (L.) Merr.) holds a unique position in global and Indian agriculture as a dual-purpose 'miracle crop'. Botanically classified as a grain legume within the family Fabaceae, it contains approximately 40-42% high-biological-value protein and 20-22% edible oil. This dual nutritional composition distinguishes it from traditional pulses (which have 20-25% protein and <2% oil) and conventional oilseeds (such as mustard or sunflower, which possess 35-45% oil but lower protein).\n\nEconomically, soybean is the bedrock of India's commercial solvent extraction industry. The extracted oil serves as a major domestic refined edible cooking oil, contributing substantially toward national self-sufficiency in fats. Simultaneously, the residual by-product\u2014de-oiled soybean cake or soybean meal (DOC)\u2014is a premium, lysine-rich protein source commanding enormous international export value and forming the primary feed ingredient for poultry, aquaculture, and livestock industries. Furthermore, the crop fixes 80-120 kg N/ha, enriches soil organic matter, and acts as a pivotal cash-generating pulse-oilseed hybrid.",
