      "questionText": "Describe the physiological phenomenon of gynophore pegging in groundnut and its agronomic implications.",
      "modelAnswer": "Pegging in groundnut (Arachis hypogaea) is an extraordinary botanical and physiological adaptation where aerial flowers produce subterranean fruits. Following successful self-pollination and fertilization in the aerial flower, the intercalary meristem located at the base of the sessile ovary becomes active. It elongates to produce a stiff, pointed, needle-like structure known as the gynophore or 'peg'.\n\nThe peg is positively geotropic, growing downward toward the soil surface at a growth rate of 0.5 to 1.0 cm per day. The tip of the peg is protected by a cap-like structure analogous to a root cap. Upon contacting the soil, the peg must penetrate 3 to 5 cm into the loose upper soil layer. Once inside the dark, humid subterranean environment, the positive geotropism ceases, the tip turns horizontal (diageotropic orientation), and the ovary swells rapidly through periclinal cell division to form the pod.\n\nAgronomically, pegging requires a friable, well-aerated sandy loam soil free of hard crusts. Heavy earthing-up or deep inter-cultivation during peg penetration must be avoided to prevent mechanical snapping of pegs and pod abortion.",
