      "syllabusTopic": "Groundnut (Arachis hypogaea): botanical classification, gynophore pegging, calcium dynamics, and aflatoxin management",
      "questionText": "Explain the physiological reason why gypsum application is indispensable at the pegging stage in groundnut.",
      "modelAnswer": "Gypsum ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$) application at 400-500 kg/ha at the flowering-to-pegging stage (35-45 DAS) is an essential agronomic intervention in groundnut cultivation due to the unique nutritional physiology of the developing pod.\n\nCalcium is a phloem-immobile plant nutrient; it translocates almost exclusively through the xylem vessel stream driven by the transpirational pull of foliage. Because groundnut pods develop completely underground in an environment with virtually 100% relative humidity, they exhibit zero transpirational pull. Consequently, calcium cannot be translocated basipetally (downward) from the leaves or roots through the vascular system into the buried gynophore and developing fruit.\n\nTherefore, the developing pod must absorb calcium directly from the surrounding soil solution through the outer epidermis of its shell. If the calcium saturation in the top 5-7 cm fruiting zone is deficient, the ovules fail to develop, leading to empty, shriveled, or blind shells known as 'pops'. Gypsum provides highly soluble calcium ($Ca^{2+}$) directly to the fruiting zone, preventing pod abortion while supplying sulfur ($S$) essential for methionine and cysteine synthesis.",
