      "modelAnswer": "Sterility Mosaic Disease (SMD), often termed the 'green plague' of pigeonpea, is one of the most destructive viral constraints to redgram production across the Indian subcontinent. It is caused by the Pigeonpea sterility mosaic emaravirus and is transmitted in a semi-persistent manner by the microscopic eriophyid mite Aceria cajani.\n\n### Characteristic Symptoms\nInfected plants exhibit severe mosaic mottling, chlorotic ring spots, and reduction in leaflet size. The vegetative growth becomes bushy, highly crowded, and pale green due to excessive axillary proliferation and shortened internodes. The most devastating symptom is complete or partial floral sterility: infected branches fail to produce floral buds, resulting in total cessation of pod set and 100% grain yield loss in early infections.\n\n### Integrated Management Strategies\n1. **Host Plant Resistance:** Cultivation of resistant or tolerant cultivars, including ICPL 87119 (Asha), BSMR 736, BSMR 853, and BDN 711.\n2. **Vector Management:** Prophylactic spraying of acaricides such as fenazaquin 10% EC (1.5 ml/L) or wettable sulfur (3 g/L) at the first sighting of mites.\n3. **Sanitation:** Roguing and destruction of infected volunteer plants and perennial hedge pigeonpeas that harbor mite vectors during off-seasons.",
