      "syllabusTopic": "Green gram (Vigna radiata) and Black gram (Vigna mungo): agronomic management, MYMV resistance, and foliar nutrition",
      "questionText": "Outline the physiological rationale and protocol for foliar application of 2% DAP in pulse crops.",
      "modelAnswer": "Grain legumes such as green gram, black gram, and soybean frequently suffer from 'monocarpic senescence' during reproductive ontogeny. As heavy pod development sets in, the developing seeds act as powerful carbohydrate sinks, monopolizing photosynthates and starving root nodules. Consequently, biological nitrogen fixation drops precipitously just when nutrient demand is highest. Furthermore, root absorption of phosphorus slows down. Foliar feeding of 2% Diammonium Phosphate (DAP) directly overcomes this nutritional bottleneck.\n\n### Protocol for 2% DAP Spray\n1. **Solution Preparation:** Dissolve 2.0 kg of fertilizer-grade DAP in 10 liters of water. Stir thoroughly and allow the suspension to stand overnight. Decant the clear supernatant liquid through a muslin cloth to eliminate insoluble inert carriers that could clog sprayer nozzles. Dilute the clear liquid to 100 liters with water.\n2. **Application Schedule:** The first foliar spray is administered at early flowering (30-35 DAS), followed by a second spray at active pod development (45-50 DAS).\n3. **Physiological Benefits:** Foliar absorption of nitrogen and phosphorus maintains canopy chlorophyll content, prolongs photosynthetic lifespan, arrests flower and pod drop, and enhances 100-seed weight by 15-20%.",
