      "syllabusTopic": "Groundnut (Arachis hypogaea): botanical classification, gynophore pegging, calcium dynamics, and aflatoxin management",
      "questionText": "Compare the Spanish bunch and Virginia runner types of groundnut across key morphological, physiological, and agronomic parameters.",
      "modelAnswer": "Spanish bunch (*Arachis hypogaea* subsp. *fastigiata*) and Virginia runner (*A. hypogaea* subsp. *hypogaea*) represent the two primary commercial classes of groundnut, differing fundamentally in botany and crop management:\n\n1. **Growth Habit and Canopy:** Spanish bunch plants grow strictly erect with compact, upright stems and central clusters of pods. Virginia runner plants exhibit prostrate, creeping vines that trail along the soil surface, with pods developing along the entire length of lateral branches.\n2. **Branching Morphology:** Spanish bunch exhibits sequential branching, wherein flowers arise directly on the main stem and adjacent secondary branches. Virginia runner exhibits alternate branching, where vegetative branch pairs strictly alternate with reproductive branch pairs, and no flowers arise on the primary central stem.\n3. **Maturity and Duration:** Spanish types mature early (90\u2013105 days), making them ideal for short-monsoon rainfed tracts. Virginia types require 120\u2013145 days to reach full maturity.\n4. **Seed Dormancy:** Spanish types lack seed dormancy; if late monsoonal rains coincide with harvest, seeds germinate directly inside mature subterranean pods (*in-situ* sprouting). Virginia types possess an innate embryonic dormancy of 30\u201360 days, safeguarding grain quality during wet harvests.\n5. **Kernel Size and Use:** Spanish cultivars produce smaller, rounder kernels with higher oil content (48\u201350%) primarily for oil crushing; Virginia types produce bold, oblong kernels (44\u201346% oil) favored for table consumption and peanut butter.",
