      "questionText": "Differentiate between Green gram (Vigna radiata) and Black gram (Vigna mungo) in terms of botanical features, grain quality, and agronomic adaptations.",
      "modelAnswer": "Green gram (*Vigna radiata*) and Black gram (*Vigna mungo*) are closely related Asiatic legumes belonging to the subgenus *Ceratotropis*, but exhibit distinct botanical, culinary, and agronomic features:\n\n1. **Vegetative Morphology:** Black gram plants are characterized by dense, coarse, brown-to-black pubescence (hairs) covering the stems, leaves, and pods. Green gram plants are less hairy, possessing smooth to sparsely pubescent vegetative surfaces with lighter green foliage.\n2. **Floral and Pod Characteristics:** Black gram produces shorter, stout, sub-erect pods with heavy bristly hairs, pointing distinctly upward or outward. Green gram produces longer, slender, cylindrical pods that hang pendulous and have smoother surfaces.\n3. **Seed Morphology and Hilum:** Black gram seeds are dull black (or rarely dark brown) with a prominent, raised, white hilum that distinctly protrudes above the seed surface. Green gram seeds are bright to dull olive green, cylindrical to globular, with a flat, non-protruding white hilum.\n4. **Culinary and Protein Utility:** Black gram flour contains unique mucilaginous arabinogalactans that provide high viscosity, foam stability, and elasticity, making it indispensable for fermented batters (idli, dosa) and papads. Green gram is exceptionally light, easily digestible, and causes minimal flatulence, making it the preferred dal for convalescents and infant weaning foods.\n5. **Agronomic Hardiness:** Black gram demonstrates superior tolerance to heavy clay soils and moisture stress, while green gram exhibits faster initial growth and better adaptation to light sandy loam soils.",
