      "questionText": "Compare the biochemical and physiological features of fast-growing Rhizobium species versus slow-growing Bradyrhizobium species.",
      "modelAnswer": "Symbiotic diazotrophs nodulating grain legumes fall into two distinct phylogenetic and physiological genera: *Rhizobium* (fast-growers) and *Bradyrhizobium* (slow-growers):\n\n1. **Growth Kinetics and Generation Time:** *Rhizobium* species (e.g., *R. leguminosarum*, *R. phaseoli*) are fast-growers with a short generation doubling time of 2 to 4 hours, forming visible colonies on yeast extract mannitol agar (YEMA) within 3 to 5 days. *Bradyrhizobium* species (e.g., *B. japonicum*) are slow-growers with doubling times exceeding 8 to 12 hours, requiring 7 to 10 days to produce noticeable colonies.\n2. **Acid-Base Reaction on Media:** Fast-growing *Rhizobium* species utilize a wide range of carbohydrates (disaccharides and hexoses), producing acid on YEMA containing bromothymol blue (turning media yellow). Slow-growing *Bradyrhizobium* prefer pentoses and produce an alkaline reaction on YEMA (turning media deep blue).\n3. **Flagellar Anatomy:** *Rhizobium* cells typically possess 2 to 6 peritrichous or subpolar flagella. *Bradyrhizobium* cells typically feature a single polar or subpolar flagellum.\n4. **Genetic Organization:** In *Rhizobium*, nodulation (*nod*) and nitrogenase (*nif*) genes are clustered on large, transmissible **symbiotic plasmids (pSym)**. In *Bradyrhizobium*, symbiotic genes are integrated directly into the stable circular **bacterial chromosome** on symbiotic islands.\n5. **Host Specificity:** *Rhizobium* species primarily nodulate temperate legumes (pea, lentil, chickpea, beans). *Bradyrhizobium* nodulates tropical legumes (soybean, cowpea, groundnut, pigeonpea).",
