      "questionText": "A farmer in Saurashtra observes that his groundnut crop has luxuriant vegetative growth and abundant yellow flowering, but pod sampling reveals over 45% empty, unfertilized shells ('pops'). Diagnose the causes and outline a remedial action plan.",
      "modelAnswer": "### 1. Diagnosis of the Problem\nThe occurrence of luxuriant vegetative foliage accompanied by high flower count but over 45% empty, blind pods ('pops') indicates acute **calcium deficiency** and potential **nitrogen-potassium-calcium imbalance** in the pod zone (fruiting zone).\n\n**Etiology:**\n- Groundnut gynophores and subterranean pods absorb calcium directly from the surrounding soil solution through the shell wall rather than through root uptake and xylem translocation.\n- If excess nitrogen fertilizer was applied, it promoted rampant vegetative canopy growth at the expense of reproductive sink filling.\n- High soil potassium ($K^+$) or magnesium ($Mg^{2+}$) in the root/pod zone competitively inhibits calcium ($Ca^{2+}$) uptake across the pod shell epidermis.\n- Sandy soils with low organic matter and low cation exchange capacity (CEC) readily leach calcium, creating an acute localized calcium starvation during active shell wall calcification (35\u201360 DAS).\n\n### 2. Remedial and Corrective Action Plan\n1. **Immediate In-Season Remediation:**\n   - Apply finely ground **agricultural gypsum ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$)** @ 400\u2013500 kg/ha directly along the crop rows (close to the fruiting zone).\n   - Follow immediately with light inter-cultivation or hoeing to incorporate gypsum into the top 3\u20135 cm soil layer around the pegs.\n   - Provide a light irrigation immediately after gypsum application; calcium must dissolve into soil moisture to be absorbed by pods. Gypsum also supplies sulfur, which enhances kernel filling.\n2. **Nutritional Adjustment:**\n   - Immediately cease all top-dressings of nitrogenous fertilizers (urea) to halt excessive vegetative flush.\n   - Spray 0.5% Borax (or 0.2% solubor) along with 1% potassium nitrate ($KNO_3$) to improve pollen fertility and facilitate carbohydrate translocation into developing kernels.\n3. **Long-Term Preventive Measures for Future Seasons:**\n   - Soil test prior to sowing to ensure calcium base saturation exceeds 15% of CEC in the top 10 cm.\n   - Apply 250 kg/ha gypsum basally and 250 kg/ha at pegging (35\u201345 DAS).\n   - Maintain friable, uncompacted soil structure by incorporating 5\u201310 t/ha FYM or compost.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Diagnostic etiology: calcium starvation in subterranean fruiting zone and K-Ca competitive inhibition",
        "Physiological mechanism of pod popping (uncalcified shell, ovule abortion)",
        "Immediate remediation: split gypsum application (400-500 kg/ha) incorporated into top 5 cm",
        "Immediate moisture scheduling to facilitate Ca2+ dissolution into soil solution",
        "Preventive soil management: pre-sowing CEC correction and dual-split gypsum schedules"
      ]
