      "questionText": "CASE STUDY: An agricultural development project in Marathwada, Maharashtra, surveyed 200 smallholder pigeonpea farmers. The survey revealed: (a) 70% of farms suffered 30-50% plant mortality from Fusarium wilt; (b) 60% suffered floral sterility from SMD; (c) average grain yield was only 650 kg/ha under monocropping. Design a 3-year holistic rehabilitation plan incorporating cropping systems, resistant genetics, and crop management.",
      "modelAnswer": "### 1. Diagnosis of Agro-Ecosystem Failure\nThe Marathwada farming community is trapped in an agronomic failure cycle caused by:\n- **Monoculture Pathology:** Continuous sole cropping of redgram has built up massive soil-borne inoculum of *Fusarium udum* (vascular wilt).\n- **Susceptible Inbreds:** Farmers cultivate outdated, susceptible farm-saved seed lots vulnerable to both *F. udum* and *Aceria cajani*-transmitted Sterility Mosaic Disease (SMD).\n- **Agronomic Inefficiency:** Traditional monoculture exposes farmers to extreme climate risks (monsoon dry spells) and poor land utilization during the 60-day initial slow vegetative phase.\n\n### 2. Year-by-Year Holistic Rehabilitation Blueprint\n\n#### Year 1: Genetic Replacement and Cropping System Transition\n- **Varietal Overhaul:** Replace all local cultivars with certified seeds of dual-resistant varieties: **ICPL 87119 (Asha)** or **BSMR 736** (immune/resistant to both Fusarium wilt and Sterility Mosaic Disease).\n- **Intercropping Shift:** Transition 100% of acreage from sole redgram to a **Soybean + Pigeonpea (4:2 or 2:1 row ratio)** or **Cotton + Pigeonpea (1:1)** intercropping system. Soybean covers the soil within 25 days, suppresses weeds, provides early cash flow in 90 days, and diversifies income.\n- **Bio-Shielding:** Treat all seeds with *Trichoderma viride* (@ 5 g/kg seed) and *Rhizobium* culture, accompanied by basal soil application of *Trichoderma*-enriched FYM (5 kg in 500 kg FYM/ha).\n\n#### Year 2: Deployment of CMS Hybrid Technology and Pest Scouting\n- **Hybrid Introduction:** Introduce high-yielding commercial CMS pigeonpea hybrids (**ICPH 2671** or **ICPH 2740**) in 50% of the demonstration clusters. These hybrids deliver a 35% yield advantage, vigorous taproots, and field tolerance to drought and wilt.\n- **Canopy Management (Nipping):** Train farmers to perform apical bud nipping at 45\u201350 DAS. This suppresses apical dominance, multiplying secondary branches and pod clusters.\n- **SMD Vector Monitoring:** Install 15 yellow sticky traps/ha and spray wettable sulfur (3 g/L) or fenazaquin (1.5 ml/L) upon early mite detection.\n\n#### Year 3: Scaling, Seed Village Concept, and Value Addition\n- **Establishment of 'Seed Villages':** Organize farmer groups into FPOs to produce certified seeds of Asha and ICPH 2671, ensuring local seed security.\n- **Crop Rotation Enforcement:** Enforce rotation with non-host cereals (Sorghum or Pearl millet); sorghum root exudates stimulate germination of *Fusarium* chlamydospores in the absence of a host, dramatically reducing wilt inoculum.\n- **Mini Dal Mills:** Install community mini dal processing units to capture local value addition, raising farm-gate realization from raw grain by 25\u201330%.\n\n### 3. Projected Outcomes\n- Elimination of wilt and SMD mortality (<5% incidence).\n- Increase in pigeonpea productivity from 650 kg/ha to 1,400\u20131,600 kg/ha, plus a bonus soybean yield of 1,800 kg/ha from intercropping, tripling net farm income.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "System pathology analysis: soil inoculum buildup under monocropping and obsolete genetics",
        "Year 1: 100% varietal replacement with dual-resistant Asha/BSMR 736 and Soybean+Pigeonpea (4:2) intercropping",
        "Year 2: commercial deployment of A4 CMS hybrids (ICPH 2671), nipping at 45 DAS, and acaricide scouting",
        "Year 3: FPO seed village creation, non-host sorghum rotation to collapse wilt spore load, and mini dal mills",
        "Target metrics: >100% yield gain and climate-resilient economic stability"
      ]
